    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A union of the Anglian kingdoms of Bernicia and Deira that stretched from the Humber to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>.</li><li>Its monasteries produced Bede, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>, the Lindisfarne Gospels and the Codex Amiatinus, the Northumbrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</li><li>Defeat at Nechtansmere in 685 ended its expansion; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Vikings</a> took York in 867 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> absorbed it in 954.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Codex Amiatinus, made at Wearmouth-Jarrow and the oldest surviving complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> Bible, was long thought to be Italian until its Northumbrian origin was identified in the 1880s.</p></aside>
    </section>

